Ladybird skips letter-spacing after a cluster that starts with a
character of a cursive script, and counts a character by its script
extensions when its own script is Common or Inherited. Blink’s
ShapeResultSpacing::ComputeSpacing() also skips it inside a cursive
script run, except at spaces, and its IsCursiveScript() lists the same
seven scripts. Its script runs take in Common and Inherited characters,
so it gives a tatweel inside an Arabic word no spacing either. Gecko’s
PropertyProvider::GetSpacingInternal() checks each cluster’s base
character with UnicodeProperties::IsCursiveScript(), which lists the
same seven scripts but reads only the Script property, and a tatweel’s
is Common — so Gecko spaces a tatweel inside an Arabic word.

Run in Chrome 157 and Firefox 151 on ten samples, both add no spacing
inside Arabic (with harakat, and with Arabic-Indic digits), Syriac,
N’Ko, or Mongolian words, and add it once at the space between two
Arabic words, as Ladybird now does. For a tatweel, Chrome adds none, as
Ladybird now does, and Firefox adds it once.
